item.description:
Did Saudi Arabia play a role in the planning and execution of the September 11 attacks of 2001? That question was raised immediately after the attack, when 15 of the 19 hijackers were revealed to be Saudi citizens, and it has only grown louder in the 25 years since as more evidence emerged of the attackers&#8217; connections to Saudi figures inside the United States.</p>
<p>We speak with <em>ProPublica</em> journalist Tim Golden, who reports that the <span class="caps">FBI</span> failed to fully investigate Omar al-Bayoumi, a Saudi intelligence asset who helped two of the hijackers as they arrived in California in early 2000. Key evidence, including a handwritten note by al-Bayoumi calculating an airplane&#8217;s trajectory to a point on the horizon, was not fully disclosed to investigators.</p>
<p>&#8220;Al-Bayoumi was incredibly important, but this evidence … sort of went missing for more than 20 years,&#8221; says Golden. &#8220;If the Saudi government is indirectly responsible, or even if this was some rogue cell of Saudis within the religious bureaucracy, we would hope that that should come out.&#8221;

We look at how post-9/11 policies and the so-called war on terror reshaped the country and the world with two Columbia faculty members who convened a two-day symposium on &#8220;The Forever War and the First Amendment.&#8221; The symposium brought together journalists, scholars, lawyers and activists to explore the legacy of the &#8220;war on terror,&#8221; specifically focused on the impact on democratic rights, including the freedoms of speech and the press.</p>
<p>First Amendment freedoms &#8220;were tested right away in the days and weeks after 9/11,&#8221; says Jameel Jaffer, legal expert and director of the Knight First Amendment Institute at Columbia University. &#8220;All of the surveillance authorities that were put in place after 9/11,&#8221; says Jaffer, &#8220;their use was initially justified by reference to foreign threats. And now, increasingly, we&#8217;re seeing the Trump administration use these things to suppress domestic dissent.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8220;If you go back to that climate after 9/11 …  newsrooms institute[d] shifts, so they started making beats for covering national security,&#8221; says Azmat Khan of the Columbia Journalism School, who won a Pulitzer Prize for exposing the massive civilian death toll from U.S.-led airstrikes in Iraq, Syria and Afghanistan. &#8220;Legacy news media in that time period was incredibly dependent on anonymous sources, sometimes unvetted intelligence, leaks to journalists that oftentimes came from an administration that was trying to lead the public to war.&#8221;

Tyson Foods, the United States&#8217; largest meatpacking and processing company, shuttered a beef processing facility in Joslin, Illinois, last month, laying off more than 2,500 primarily immigrant workers &#8220;virtually overnight.&#8221; Although Tyson Foods <span class="caps">CEO</span> and President Donnie King made over $34 million last year, nearly 800 times more than the median Tyson worker, the company cites a historic 75-year low in the U.S. cattle herd as a primary reason for the closure.</p>
<p>We speak to Caitlyn Clark, director of the group Essential Workers for Democracy, which is working to institute democratic reforms within the United Food and Commercial Workers International Union (<span class="caps">UFCW</span>), a labor union that represents more than a million workers across the United States. These workers include the former Tyson plant meatpackers, who worked in &#8220;some of the most dangerous jobs in America&#8221; while making &#8220;billions of dollars in profit for Tyson.&#8221; Clark ascribes the U.S. cattle shortage to climate change-induced drought in the American West, rising prices for diesel and fertilizer amid the U.S. war on Iran and years of corporate consolidation that has driven down profits for cattle ranchers.</p>
<p>Since they were suddenly laid off, former Joslin plant workers have been protesting outside the shuttered plant, calling on Tyson &#8220;to extend their severance pay, and calling on Illinois Governor JB Pritzker to intervene to help keep the plant open,&#8221; Clark tells us. &#8220;We need to take on the bosses and be serious about the level of inequality and exploitation that working people are facing today,&#8221; she adds. &#8220;Working people need more, they deserve more, and they&#8217;re ready to fight for it. It&#8217;s time that our union leadership catch up.&#8221;

A federal judge has ruled that the Trump administration violated the constitutional rights of international students in seeking to deport them for their pro-Palestinian activism and criticism of Israel. The ruling is the result of a lawsuit filed by <em>The Stanford Daily</em>, the student newspaper at Stanford University, which claimed that some of its writers on student visas declined to report on topics related to Israel and Palestine, or requested their published articles be removed from the paper&#8217;s website, for fear of deportation.</p>
<p>&#8220;What we were hearing from the noncitizens in our newsroom and from noncitizens across campus — current students, former students, alumni — was that they were genuinely frightened because of what they saw in the headlines, what they saw happen to Rümeysa Öztürk,&#8221; says George Porteous, editor-in-chief of <em>The Stanford Daily</em>. Rümeysa Öztürk is the former Tufts University graduate student arrested and detained by <span class="caps">ICE</span> after co-authoring an op-ed encouraging Tufts to divest from Israel in her university&#8217;s student newspaper.</p>
<p>&#8220;The chill is the point,&#8221; adds Will Creeley, legal director at the Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression, or <span class="caps">FIRE</span>, the organization that represented <em>The Stanford Daily</em> in court. &#8220;That&#8217;s a First Amendment violation every day of the week. It&#8217;s flatly un-American.&#8221;

More than 900 people in Nepal are confirmed dead after a massive wall of water, mud and debris crashed through a Himalayan river valley on the border with Tibet. More than 4,200 people are missing in Nepal and hundreds more in Tibet. Families are searching hospitals for loved ones, and crews have buried hundreds of unidentified bodies in shallow graves. Rescue efforts are also underway for more than 900 hydropower workers, believed to be trapped in more than a dozen underground tunnels blocked by debris. The cause of the flash flooding is believed to have been the collapse of a glacier in the mountains, leading a wave of water, rocks and debris to carve its way through the valley.</p>
<p>&#8220;This is a catastrophe of unimaginable proportions,&#8221; says journalist Kunda Dixit, speaking to <em>Democracy Now!</em> from the Nepalese capital of Kathmandu.</p>
<p>&#8220;We used to say that the glaciers are melting in the Himalaya. Now it seems like the mountains are cracking up,&#8221; says Dixit. &#8220;This has sort of redefined what global warming and climate change is doing, not just to Nepal and the Himalaya, but to the planet.&#8221;
